====References====
* '''2018''', Robert McColl Millar, ''[https://www.google.com/books/edition/Modern_Scots/XtdJEAAAQBAJ Modern Scots]: An Analytical Survey'', pages 13-14:
*: [The] Scots dialects of the Black Isle, a promontory to the north of Inverness, were largely confined to two villages, Cromarty and Avoch, which are not fully connected to the North- East Scots- speaking regions to the east of Inverness {{...}} The Black Isle dialects (North Northern B) shared much with their Caithness equivalents. With one feature, however, they stood alone, not only in the North or even Scotland, but in the English-speaking world. {{...|The loss of /h/ in all positions is a very unusual feature anywhere north of the River Tyne in the North-East of England; indeed it is essentially peculiar to the Black Isle. What is much more striking for that region, however, is that}} the &lt;wh&gt; words were not replaced by /f/, as is the case with the other Northern dialects, but by ''nothing''. The Scots equivalent to English ''what'', which is ''fit'' or ''fat'' in the rest of the Scots-speaking North, was ''at'' in Cromarty and Avoch. {{...}} a good case could be made for the last speaker of archetypically 'Black Isle Scots' dying in 2012.

